\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{A positive coefficient expansion on SU(2)}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{f} be the defining two-dimensional representation of
#{SU(2)} and let #{\beta>0}. The function
##{k(g)=\ue^{\beta\chi_f(g)}}
is continuous, central, strictly positive and inversion-symmetric.
For each integer #{N\ge0} and each irreducible #{\rho}, write
#{m_\rho(n)} for the multiplicity of #{\rho} in #{f^{\otimes n}} and
##{c_{\rho,N}=\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac{\beta^n}{n!}m_\rho(n).}
Each tensor power contributes a finite character sum,
##{\chi_f(g)^n=\sum_\rho m_\rho(n)\chi_\rho(g),
 \qquad \sum_\rho m_\rho(n)d_\rho=2^n.}
The Taylor polynomial of degree #{N} therefore expands as
##{\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac{\beta^n}{n!}\chi_f(g)^n
 =\sum_\rho c_{\rho,N}\chi_\rho(g),}
and the dimension-weighted sum of the coefficients is the partial
sum of the exponential,
##{\sum_\rho d_\rho c_{\rho,N}
 =\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac{(2\beta)^n}{n!}.}
Lagrange's form of the remainder gives a real #{\xi} between #{0}
and #{2\beta} such that
##{\ue^{2\beta}
 =\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac{(2\beta)^n}{n!}
 +\ue^{\xi}\frac{(2\beta)^{N+1}}{(N+1)!}.}
For each #{g} there is a real number #{\eta} between #{0} and
#{\beta\chi_f(g)} such that
##{k(g)=\sum_{n=0}^{N}\frac{(\beta\chi_f(g))^n}{n!}
 +\ue^{\eta}\frac{(\beta\chi_f(g))^{N+1}}{(N+1)!}.}
The irreducible representations with
#{c_{\rho,N}>0} form a finite set #{F}, and
#{k_N=\sum_{\rho\in F}c_{\rho,N}\chi_\rho} is the truncation
#{k_F} of \ref{fdip-007Z} with coefficients
#{a_\rho=c_{\rho,N}/d_\rho}. For each irreducible #{\rho} there is
a finite degree at which it occurs, and #{c_{\rho,N}>0} for every
larger truncation degree.}

\proof{
\p{Use the ordered matrix chart of \ref{fdip-007G}. In that chart
an element of #{SU(2)} is
##{g=\begin{pmatrix}z&w\\-\overline w&\overline z\end{pmatrix},
 \qquad |z|^2+|w|^2=1.}
Hence #{\chi_f(g)=2\operatorname{Re}z} is real and lies in
#{[-2,2]}. Also
#{\chi_f(g^{-1})=\chi_f(g)}, and #{k} is continuous, central
and strictly positive because it is an exponential of a real
value. Every tensor power is unitary; invariant orthogonal
complements decompose it into irreducibles. Evaluating the
character identity at the identity gives
#{\sum_\rho m_\rho(n)d_\rho=2^n}. Only finitely many multiplicities
are nonzero. Multiplying by #{\beta^n/n!} and summing through
degree #{N} gives the displayed polynomial and the partial sum
#{\sum_\rho d_\rho c_{\rho,N}}.}

\p{For #{\psi(s)=\ue^s}, Taylor expansion at #{0} with Lagrange
remainder produces, at #{s=2\beta}, a point #{\xi} between #{0}
and #{2\beta} with the displayed scalar identity. The same
expansion at #{s=\beta\chi_f(g)} produces a point #{\eta} between
#{0} and #{\beta\chi_f(g)} and the displayed pointwise identity
for #{k(g)}. Since #{\beta>0} and each multiplicity is nonnegative,
#{c_{\rho,N}} is a sum of nonnegative terms.}

\p{Consider the algebra of finite linear combinations of matrix
coefficients of the powers #{f^{\otimes n}}, including #{n=0}.
Products are coefficients of tensor products, so this is a unital
algebra of continuous functions. The entries of #{g} distinguish
its points. Their complex conjugates are again entries up to a
sign, by the displayed matrix form, so the algebra is closed under
complex conjugation. The complex Stone–Weierstrass theorem makes
it uniformly dense in #{C(SU(2),\mathbb C)}. Here that analytic
theorem is used as a cited statement: a conjugation-closed unital
subalgebra of continuous complex functions on a compact space is
dense if it separates points. Its exact formal statement is
\leanref{ContinuousMap.starSubalgebra_topologicalClosure_eq_top_of_separatesPoints}.
The reference supplies this density theorem, not the representation
argument that follows.}

\p{If an irreducible #{\rho} occurred in no tensor power, Schur
orthogonality from the [Peter–Weyl theorem](fdip-007C) would make
all its matrix coefficients orthogonal to this algebra. Choose a
nonzero such coefficient #{u}. Uniform density gives algebra
functions converging uniformly, hence in normalized Haar #{L^2},
to #{u}. Orthogonality would then give #{\|u\|_2^2=0}, a
contradiction. Thus #{m_\rho(n)>0} for some finite #{n}. For every
truncation degree #{N\ge n}, the corresponding term is positive,
so #{c_{\rho,N}>0}. The representations with positive coefficient
at a fixed #{N} are among the irreducible summands of
#{f^{\otimes 0},\ldots,f^{\otimes N}}, a finite list. Self-duality
of the irreducible representations of #{SU(2)} closes that list
under duals.}
}

\p{Remark. Continuum comparison. The convergent series is not the
statement above. Summing every degree gives
##{c_\rho=\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{\beta^n}{n!}m_\rho(n),\qquad
 a_\rho=\frac{c_\rho}{d_\rho},\qquad
 \sum_\rho d_\rho^2a_\rho=\ue^{2\beta}.}
The scalar remainder tends to #{0} as #{N} increases, and
#{|\chi_\rho(g)|\le d_\rho} lets the character series of #{k} be
rearranged uniformly. The resulting coefficients are positive for
every #{\rho}, by the finite occurrence proved above. This is the
expansion compared with
\citet{Proposition 1, p. 288}{luscher1977transfer}.}
